#nfsh .a-rail a:hover{color:var(--ink)}
#nfsh .a-rail a[aria-current="true"]{color:var(--ink);font-weight:600;border-left-color:var(--ink)}
/* ---- in-article figures ----
 The photos are frames from the film, 5880 px PNGs on the CDN. They are served
 through Shopify's ?width= transform (the CDN answers WebP on Vary: Accept, so a
 900 px frame is ~28 KB instead of 8-18 MB) and held to the text column so they
 stay modest — the source quality does not survive being blown up.
 3:2 on desktop, 4:3 on mobile: the frames are 1.78-2.21 panoramas, so a portrait
 crop would throw away most of the picture, while 4:3 gives them real presence on
 a phone and keeps every subject (all of them are centre-framed). */
#nfsh .a-fig{margin-top:34px}
#nfsh .a-fig img{width:100%;aspect-ratio:3/2;object-fit:cover;background:var(--soft)}
/* The four landscapes as one low strip, where the text says the samples went to
 the Dolomites. They were four separate 1116 px figures spread over the article,
 which added ~2000 px of scroll — and at that width these video stills show their
 grain. Small is genuinely kinder to them: 270 px cells hide the noise and the
 whole set costs 180 px of page instead of 2200. */
#nfsh .a-strip{grid-column:1 / -1;margin-top:34px;display:grid;
 grid-template-columns:repeat(3,minmax(0,1fr));gap:12px}
/* Four frames in the markup: a phone shows a clean 2x2, desktop shows three in a row.
 The fourth is display:none there — and because it is loading="lazy" it is never in
 a viewport on desktop, so it is not downloaded either. */
#nfsh .a-strip img:nth-child(4){display:none}
#nfsh .a-strip img{width:100%;aspect-ratio:3/2;object-fit:cover;background:var(--soft)}
/* photo + the two models it belongs to, side by side — the figure and the card used
 to stack, which cost ~900 px of scroll for one argument */
/* The CARD sets the height and the photo fills it exactly. Letting the photo keep an
 intrinsic ratio put the leftover space inside the card instead — the same dead air,
 just moved. Absolute fill means neither column can ever have a gap, in any language.
 min-height is only a floor for a very short card. */
#nfsh .a-combo{grid-column:1 / -1;margin-top:34px;display:grid;
 grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:24px;align-items:stretch}
#nfsh .a-combo > figure{position:relative;min-height:240px}
#nfsh .a-combo > figure img{position:absolute;inset:0;width:100%;height:100%;
 object-fit:cover;background:var(--soft)}
/* the card brings its own 34px margin-top, which pushed it below the photo */
#nfsh .a-combo .a-prod{margin-top:0}
/* ---- shoppable pair card: women's + men's of the model the text just named ---- */
#nfsh .a-prod{margin-top:34px;border:1px solid var(--line);padding:18px;
 transition:border-color .15s ease,box-shadow .15s ease}
#nfsh .a-prod .s-rows{margin-top:0}
#nfsh .a-prod:hover{border-color:#c9c9c9;box-shadow:0 2px 14px rgba(17,17,17,.05)}
/* The label used to float at the card's left edge, directly above the thumbnail
 column — so it sat over a picture rather than over the names it labels, and read as
 a stray tag. A hairline under it makes it the card's header instead, using the same
 device that already separates the two rows: rule, row, rule, row. */
#nfsh .a-prod-kicker{display:block;font-size:11px !important;font-weight:700;
 letter-spacing:.11em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--grey);
 line-height:1 !important;padding-bottom:13px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)}
