 <h2 id="nfsh-s2" data-nfsh-toc data-t="h2_2">An idea can arrive in a car park</h2>
 <p data-t="s2p1">The design of the Nanda did not come out of a single brainstorming session either.</p>
 <p data-t="s2p2">Our designer calls this process “creative fermentation” — the period when all the research, discussions and inputs gradually come together until a concrete idea emerges from them.</p>
 <p data-t="s2p3">In the Nanda’s case it arrived on the way to a ski trip. When the car pulled up at the resort, skiing was set aside. Luckily there was an iPad in the boot, and she spent the next two hours sketching in the car park.</p>
 <figure class="a-fig">
 <img src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/1009/2838/9452/files/sketch_photo.png?v=1788260389&amp;width=640" srcset="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/1009/2838/9452/files/sketch_photo.png?v=1788260389&amp;width=420 420w, https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/1009/2838/9452/files/sketch_photo.png?v=1788260389&amp;width=640 640w, https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/1009/2838/9452/files/sketch_photo.png?v=1788260389&amp;width=900 900w, https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/1009/2838/9452/files/sketch_photo.png?v=1788260389&amp;width=1200 1200w, https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/1009/2838/9452/files/sketch_photo.png?v=1788260389&amp;width=1400 1400w" sizes="(min-width:1024px) 675px, calc(100vw - 48px)" width="1200" height="800"
 alt="A hand sketching a shoe on a tablet, the drawing laid over the frame" data-t-alt="alt_sketch" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
 </figure>
 <p data-t="s2p4">A sketch, however, is only where developing a shoe begins.</p>
 <h2 id="nfsh-s3" data-nfsh-toc data-t="h2_3">A sample is not a finished shoe</h2>
 <p data-t="s3p1">Before a model makes it into the collection, practically everything can change — the construction of the tongue, the lacing, the materials and the outsole.</p>
 <p data-t="s3p2">With the barefoot models, for example, we compared two different outsoles. At first glance one had a more pronounced structure and tread pattern, so it might seem that this would be the one to grip better on the trail. Testing showed the opposite.</p>
 <p data-t="s3p3"><strong>Grip is not determined only by what you can see on the outsole.</strong> The composition of the rubber compound and how it behaves on a particular surface matter too. That is also why we use Vibram outsoles and work with different compounds depending on the type of footwear — for example Vibram Megagrip, with high grip even on wet rock, or XS Trek Evo, which suits classic hiking and longer trekking.</p>
 <p data-t="s3p4">The insole is a similarly inconspicuous detail. In our models we use Ortholite, which holds its shape even with long-term use. On a hike lasting several hours it is not only the headline technical parameters that decide. Comfort and foot fatigue also depend on how the shoe fits, how much it weighs and what happens inside it after kilometres of walking.</p>
 <h2 id="nfsh-s4" data-nfsh-toc data-t="h2_4">Testing means actually using the shoes</h2>
 <p data-t="s4p1">That is why samples do not stay in the office. With some of them we try the opposite — to wear them as close to the limit of what they can do as possible.</p>
 <p data-t="s4p2">The shoes were tested by our in-house team and by people for whom the mountains are a natural environment — high-altitude porters, mountain rescuers and testers who took them to the Tatras, the Alps, the Andes and Patagonia.</p>
 <figure class="a-fig">
 <img src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/1009/2838/9452/files/nature_with_testing_our_boots.png?v=1788260393&amp;width=640" srcset="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/1009/2838/9452/files/nature_with_testing_our_boots.png?v=1788260393&amp;width=420 420w, https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/1009/2838/9452/files/nature_with_testing_our_boots.png?v=1788260393&amp;width=640 640w, https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/1009/2838/9452/files/nature_with_testing_our_boots.png?v=1788260393&amp;width=900 900w, https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/1009/2838/9452/files/nature_with_testing_our_boots.png?v=1788260393&amp;width=1200 1200w, https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/1009/2838/9452/files/nature_with_testing_our_boots.png?v=1788260393&amp;width=1400 1400w" sizes="(min-width:1024px) 675px, calc(100vw - 48px)" width="1200" height="800"
 alt="Boots left at the entrance of a tent, a mountain ridge behind at dawn" data-t-alt="alt_testing" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
 </figure>
 <p data-t="s4p3">And they did not always confirm what we would have expected.</p>
 <p data-t="s4p4">Under a heavier load, for instance, it might seem obvious to reach for a high hiking boot. Yet when the mountain porters could choose a model to test, several of them preferred low approach or hiking shoes. An experienced hiker with a strong ankle may need something different from a shoe than someone who only heads into the mountains occasionally.</p>
 <p data-t="s4p5">That is also why, in our view, there is no single “best hiking boot”. There is a shoe that suits a particular person, terrain and use.</p>
 <h2 id="nfsh-s5" data-nfsh-toc data-t="h2_5">From multisport to barefoot hiking</h2>
 <p data-t="s5p1">Step by step, a collection came together for different ways of moving outdoors: the multisport Nanda and Nanga models, classic hiking footwear, approach shoes for more technical rocky terrain, lightweight models for travel and everyday outdoor wear, and the Danda and Dorje barefoot shoes.</p>
